  exp: "In acetaminophen overdose, the normal glucuronidation and sulfation pathways become saturated. The excess drug is metabolized by Cytochrome P450 into N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine (NAPQI), a highly reactive and toxic electrophile. Normally, NAPQI is neutralized by hepatic glutathione. In an overdose, glutathione is depleted, and free NAPQI rapidly binds to hepatocellular proteins, causing massive centrilobular necrosis (massive transaminitis). N-acetylcysteine (NAC) acts as the specific antidote by replenishing hepatic glutathione stores.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Oxalic acid; Fomepizole):</strong> Oxalic acid is the toxic metabolite of ethylene glycol (antifreeze) poisoning, treated with Fomepizole.<br>• <strong>Option B (Formic acid; Ethanol):</strong> Formic acid is the toxic metabolite of methanol poisoning, which causes optic neuropathy (blindness) and is treated with Fomepizole or Ethanol.<br>• <strong>Option D (Phosgene; Sodium thiosulfate):</strong> Phosgene is a toxic gas. Sodium thiosulfate is the antidote for cyanide poisoning."
