  chapter: "Toxicology",
  level: "Hard",
  q: "A 24-year-old female with a history of depression is found unresponsive with an empty bottle of amitriptyline. Vital signs show HR 130 bpm, BP 85/50 mmHg. ECG reveals sinus tachycardia with a QRS complex duration of 140 msec (normal < 120) and a rightward terminal axis. What is the most appropriate initial targeted pharmacological therapy to prevent lethal arrhythmias in this patient?",
  opts: ["Intravenous Amiodarone", "Intravenous Sodium Bicarbonate", "Intravenous Physostigmine", "Intravenous Magnesium Sulfate"],
  ans: 1,
  exp: "The patient is presenting with a Tricyclic Antidepressant (TCA) overdose. TCAs block cardiac fast sodium channels, leading to a widened QRS complex (>100 msec indicates risk of arrhythmias, >160 msec indicates risk of seizures) and right axis deviation of the terminal QRS (prominent R wave in aVR). The definitive treatment to overcome the sodium channel blockade and reverse the QRS widening is Intravenous Sodium Bicarbonate. It works by providing a massive sodium load and inducing an alkalosis, which favors the unbinding of the TCA from the sodium channel.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Intravenous Amiodarone):</strong> Antiarrhythmics like Amiodarone (Class III) and especially Class Ia or Ic drugs will further block sodium/potassium channels, exacerbating the TCA toxicity and increasing mortality.<br>• <strong>Option C (Intravenous Physostigmine):</strong> While TCAs have anticholinergic effects, physostigmine (a reversible AChE inhibitor) is strictly contraindicated in TCA overdose because it can precipitate cardiac arrest and seizures in this setting.<br>• <strong>Option D (Intravenous Magnesium Sulfate):</strong> Used for Torsades de pointes (caused by QT prolongation). While TCAs can prolong QT, QRS widening is the primary predictor of immediate mortality and is treated with Bicarbonate."
